title: 'statement-store: restore the retention tracks at startup and check the size limit on a retrack'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    A node that runs with `--statement-v2-dht` restores the retention track of every transient
    and explicit-only statement from the account index at startup. Before, the tracks lived only
    in memory, so after a restart the retention sweep saw nothing: the statements stayed on disk
    until they expired and kept counting toward the size limit of their track. Transient
    statements found at startup wait for propagation again, so a peer may receive one twice.
    The first sweep after a restart removes the explicit-only statements that no affinity
    covers, among them the statements of a database older than version 3.

    The retention sweep also checks the size limit of the track that it moves a statement to.
    While that track is full, the statement stays on its current track and the next sweep tries
    again. Before, the move could push the track above its limit, which then rejected every new
    statement for it.
crates:
- name: sc-statement-store
  bump: patch
